@page "/components/container"

<PageOutlet Url="components/container"
            Title="Container"
            Description="The BitContainer component centers the content of a page in a column with a maximum width and side gutters that grow with the width of the window." />
<div>
    <DemoPage Name="Container"
              Description="The BitContainer component centers the content of a page in a column with a maximum width and side gutters: the block every site otherwise copies into its pages (a max-width, margin-inline: auto and a padding per breakpoint). It takes the whole width it is given up to MaxWidth (80rem by default, gutters included), centers itself in what is left, and pads its content with a gutter that grows from a phone to a desktop unless NoGutter removes it."
              Parameters="componentParameters"
              CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
              GitHubUrl="Layouts/Container/BitContainer.cs"
              GitHubDemoUrl="Layouts/Container/BitContainerDemo.razor">
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
            <div class="example-desc">
                The container fills the gray band up to its maximum width and keeps its content off the edges with the
                gutters, the tinted strips on its two sides. The gutter is <code>spacing(2)</code> on a phone,
                <code>spacing(3)</code> from the <b>sm</b> breakpoint (600px) and <code>spacing(4)</code> from <b>md</b>
                (960px): resize the window to watch it step.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer Class="container">
                        <div class="content">The content of the page</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="MaxWidth" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" Id="example2">
            <div class="example-desc">
                <b>MaxWidth</b> takes any CSS length and caps the container at it, centering it in the room that is left.
                The width includes the gutters, so two containers of the same width line their content up. <code>none</code>
                lets the container span the whole width it is given and keep only its gutters.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer MaxWidth="480px" Class="container">
                        <div class="content">480px</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer MaxWidth="40rem" Class="container">
                        <div class="content">40rem</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer MaxWidth="none" Class="container">
                        <div class="content">none</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="NoGutter" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" Id="example3">
            <div class="example-desc">
                <b>NoGutter</b> removes the inline paddings, so the content spans the full width of the container while it is
                still capped and centered - for a content that brings its own inset, such as a card or an image.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer MaxWidth="480px" NoGutter Class="container">
                        <div class="content">No gutter</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Full-width bands" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" Id="example4">
            <div class="example-desc">
                A band of the page that runs edge to edge - a colored hero, a footer - is an element of its own with a
                container inside it, so the band keeps the full width while its content lines up with the rest of the page.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="hero">
                    <BitContainer MaxWidth="560px">
                        <div class="hero-title">A full-width hero</div>
                        <div>Its text lines up with the section below.</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
                <BitContainer MaxWidth="560px" Class="section">
                    The section under the hero, in a container of the same width.
                </BitContainer>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Element" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
            <div class="example-desc">
                <b>Element</b> swaps the rendered <code>div</code> for another tag: a <code>main</code> for the main content
                of the page, a <code>section</code> or an <code>article</code> for a region of it. Only the semantics change,
                so a landmark is worth naming with an <b>AriaLabel</b>.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer Element="section" AriaLabel="Latest news" MaxWidth="480px" Class="container">
                        <div class="content">A section landmark</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div class="example-desc">
                <b>BitParams</b> hands a <b>BitContainerParams</b> to every container under it, so a page sets its column once.
                The values are defaults, not overrides: a container that sets a parameter itself keeps its own.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <BitParams Parameters="@containerParams">
                    <div class="band">
                        <BitContainer Class="container">
                            <div class="content">Cascaded 480px</div>
                        </BitContainer>
                    </div>
                    <br />
                    <div class="band">
                        <BitContainer MaxWidth="320px" Class="container">
                            <div class="content">Its own 320px</div>
                        </BitContainer>
                    </div>
                </BitParams>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" Id="example7">
            <div class="example-desc">
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the root. The public variables restyle the default of every container
                they reach: <code>--bit-Container-max-width</code>, <code>--bit-Container-padding-inline</code> (a gutter set
                there holds at every width) and <code>--bit-Container-padding-block</code>. A <b>MaxWidth</b> or a
                <b>NoGutter</b> written on a container still wins over them.
            </div>
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer Style="--bit-Container-max-width:400px;--bit-Container-padding-inline:3rem;--bit-Container-padding-block:1rem" Class="container">
                        <div class="content">Variables on Style</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="band">
                    <BitContainer MaxWidth="400px" Class="container custom-class">
                        <div class="content">Class</div>
                    </BitContainer>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </DemoPage>
</div>
